I supervise pre-thesis and thesis projects for students in the School of
Computer Science and Engineering. This page explains my research areas, the
topics available, and how supervision is organised.

== Co-supervision

Some projects are co-supervised with another advisor. In such cases, the project
scope is agreed between me and the co-advisor. If you would like to be
considered for a co-supervised project, please mention this in your message.

Current co-advisors:

- *Dr. Toan-Van Nguyen* — San Diego State University, USA. /Wireless
  communications, artificial intelligence, signal processing, wireless
  security/. \n [vannguyentoan@gmail.com]

- *Assoc. Prof. Tung Kieu* — Aalborg University, Denmark. /Data mining, machine
  learning, spatio-temporal data, time-series analysis/. \n [tungkvt@cs.aau.dk]

Additional collaborator:

- *M.Sc. Nguyen Trung Nghia* — International University, Vietnam.
  \n [ntnghia@hcmiu.edu.vn]

== What I work on

My current research interests include deep learning, federated learning, edge
inference, collaborative inference, semantic communication, large language
models (LLMs), distributed learning, deep reinforcement learning for networks,
and wireless and mobile networking with the Internet of Things.

A practical way to find a topic is to review the two or three most recent papers
on the [publications.html Publications] page and consider which one you would
like to understand deeply enough to extend.

== Open topics

- *Deep Learning and Large Language Models:* applications in network
  optimization and semantic communication.
- *Federated Learning and Collaborative Inference:* distributed training and
  inference across resource-constrained edge devices.
- *Edge Intelligence and Semantic Communication:* transmitting semantic meaning
  rather than raw bits over limited bandwidth.
- *Deep Reinforcement Learning for Networks:* learning control policies for
  wireless and mobile networking without accurate models.
- *Network Optimization and Game Theory:* resource allocation and network
  utility maximization.

== How we work

- One individual meeting each week, plus a group seminar.
- You are expected to read actively, document your understanding, and present
  your work regularly — including during weeks when progress is limited.
- The goal is a defensible result: a clear research question, a sound method,
  and supporting evidence.